#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa11f230-ac01-54c6-a241-9eb830650515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368950} {:answer/id #uuid "444b69f8-1cac-5f17-9963-b92e89f8ec0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368951} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0c2a5a-5cf5-5c75-808a-065f807aad3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368952} {:answer/id #uuid "124f7a70-3b63-5ab2-a4c5-037f5441d98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368953} {:answer/id #uuid "b27177c1-e96c-5686-be29-b410d18356a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "444b69f8-1cac-5f17-9963-b92e89f8ec0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368951} :answer-field/id #uuid "e86b64bc-7b59-5c75-87d1-36e710ec3802" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a7147e0-2667-5230-a581-5d8792b92824" :question/math-academy-id "q-98642" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cff9a8967582489286a62b78234e147684d1e453c20967fe1190cf515afe246f.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}(y+3)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y(y+3)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+3y$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-3,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+3).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)={y}^{2}(y+3),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-3,0.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-3,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=y(y+3)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=y(y+3)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-3,0.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,0).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+3y.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=y(y+3),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-3,0.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-3,0).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0adf3855-8b8e-50f0-87c2-5ad31db132b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368957} {:answer/id #uuid "2376622c-effa-5c4d-9f39-6c8225c77917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368958} {:answer/id #uuid "11a8816d-6cad-59ac-91b1-a8b0b37f8344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368959} {:answer/id #uuid "7d69f29e-f9b7-5042-9707-68740332f104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368960} {:answer/id #uuid "d00a52ec-f698-5672-92af-792e82f00b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d00a52ec-f698-5672-92af-792e82f00b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368961} :answer-field/id #uuid "84cf558a-bb8d-52c9-9e7e-54cfb455084b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfb99b4c-3f9c-571a-8cfe-9d0080c591ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-98647" :question/problem "![](images/a7/a7f21df22875efb2457fcf82e616778c9f61f77843b6969c964a293f9e113a5a.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)(y-4)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-6y+8$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2}+6y-8$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=2$ and $y=4.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (2,4).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=(y-2)(y-4)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-2)(y-4)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (2,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-6y+8.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y-2)(y-4),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for  $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ and $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (2,4).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II has the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=-{y}^{2}+6y-8.$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-(y-2)(y-4),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (2,4).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083201 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cc7e89f-d22e-5fbb-a9dd-d145070270f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368971} {:answer/id #uuid "580976b8-b37c-5a64-a98c-ea4717754d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368972} {:answer/id #uuid "6b589691-e0b9-535e-8234-ba50ef3364a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368973} {:answer/id #uuid "d335b0bb-2166-58cf-b5b9-f0203c1ebcaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368974} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7f5aa4-bd8d-52e9-9abd-02aed92c23ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d335b0bb-2166-58cf-b5b9-f0203c1ebcaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368974} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e0d9dea-29ca-5077-8ef9-85315fc69e10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebe5f91b-e612-5062-b7df-947a793c33b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-101734" :question/problem "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-5}]}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2\\sqrt{6-5}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}\\,{x}^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{1/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt[3]{a}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals I and II are divergent."}]}
